// Inline assembly, for freestanding programs: a boot stub, a system register, a
// barrier the language has no word for.
//
//     import std.asm
//
//     asm("nop")
//     asmWrite("msr tpidr_el0, $0", 12345)
//     let level: U64 = asmRead("mrs $0, CurrentEL")
//
// **The text must be a string literal**, because it is part of the instruction
// stream: it is assembled where it is written, not passed to a function. That is
// why these three are not ordinary functions -- the compiler lowers a call to one
// in place (the declarations below only give the names, and the types of their
// arguments, something to resolve against; none of them is ever run). The text is
// LLVM's inline-assembly syntax, AT&T on x86: `$$` writes a literal `$` and `\n`
// separates instructions. `$0` is the value `asmRead` answers and the operand
// `asmWrite` takes. Memory is treated as clobbered, so a load or store is never
// moved across one of these; registers other than `$0` are yours to preserve.

// Runs the instructions.
public fn asm(text: String) { }

// Runs them and answers `$0`.
public fn asmRead(text: String) -> U64 { return 0 }

// Runs them with `$0` set to `value`.
public fn asmWrite(text: String, value: U64) { }

// What a register operand can be, for the declarations below. The compiler checks the real
// operands where it lowers the call, and also accepts a typed pointer.
public typelist AsmOperand { U8, I8, U16, I16, U32, U64, I64, Int, Usize, Isize, Ptr }

// Several operands, and the registers the text changes. This is also how a CPU instruction the
// language has no word for is reached, a hardware CRC step for one:
//
//     fn crc32c(crc: U32, value: U32) -> U32 {
//         return asmOpsOut(".arch_extension crc\ncrc32cw \${0:w}, \${1:w}, \${2:w}", "", crc, value) as U32
//     }
//
// (`\$` keeps Prismio from reading `${` as an interpolation; `${0:w}` is LLVM's name for the
// 32-bit view of the register, and `.arch_extension` lets the assembler accept the instruction
// whatever the target's baseline is.) Which instruction is a matter of the target, so a function
// like this belongs in a module built for that architecture alone.
//
//     asmOps("mov x8, $0\nmov x0, $1\nsvc #0", "x0,x8", number, argument)
//     let sum = asmOpsOut("add $0, $1, $2", "", a, b)
//
// The operands follow the two string literals and are `$0`, `$1`.. in `asmOps`, and `$1`.. in
// `asmOpsOut`, whose `$0` is the result it answers. Each is an integer of any width or a pointer,
// held in a register of the compiler's choosing, so the text moves it where it needs it. The
// second literal lists, comma-separated, the registers the text overwrites beyond its operands
// ("x0,x8,cc"; "" for none), because LLVM must not keep a live value in one. Memory is always
// clobbered. Neither is an ordinary function (see the header): the compiler lowers a call in place.
public fn asmOps<Ts: AsmOperand>(text: String, clobbers: String, ...operands: Ts) { }

// `asmOps` with a result in `$0`.
public fn asmOpsOut<Ts: AsmOperand>(text: String, clobbers: String, ...operands: Ts) -> U64 { return 0 }
